WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.856 --> 00:00:05.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:06.440
Hello.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.450
My name is Alan Feder.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.990
And I'm a Principal Data
Scientist at Invesco.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:15.170
My talk today is about
categorical embeddings

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:18.230
in the embed
package in R. If you

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.720
are trying to create
a predictive model,

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.740
you'd really like to use all of
the variables in your data set,

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:27.590
as that's what's
likely to create

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:31.820
the most accurate and
successful predictions.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:34.370
But you may soon
run into a problem

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.280
because many of the
functions within R,

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.650
that are used to
create these models

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:44.690
require that all of the input
data are continuous numbers.

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:48.020
Now this makes sense because
many of the computations that

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.960
go on in the background work
on linear algebra and matrices,

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.650
which inherently can only
have numbers in them.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:57.860
But that might be a problem for
you because some of your data

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.650
may be categorical
variables and you don't

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:02.780
want to have to drop them.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:07.700
You might try to replace
each category with a number--

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.930
1, 2, 3, which might work
if your data is ordinal.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:16.220
But if your data doesn't have
any inherent order to it,

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:17.840
this might not work.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:20.570
If one of your
categories is color,

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.220
how are you going
to choose numbers

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:27.200
for black, brown,
yellow or green.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.080
You might try to
create dummy variables

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:35.600
but this actually can cause
big data problems really quick.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.140
Dummy variables will
create a new variable

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:42.110
for each level of your data.

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:46.010
And for example, if
one of your categories

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.380
is United States
zip code, you're

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.310
about to add 42,000 new
variables to your data

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:55.400
and that's probably
not what you want.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:59.060
That leads me to
categorical embeddings.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:03.470
If you're familiar with Word
embeddings, such as Word2Vec.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:06.590
This is actually a very
similar idea except instead

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:10.070
of an embedding for
each Word in language,

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:16.300
you're embedding each
category in your data.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.540
What happens is that
every category is replaced

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.250
by a vector of numbers.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.670
The vector can be length, 4
can be length, 15-- it really

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:26.470
depends on the situation.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:31.240
But these numbers will replace
your categorical variable

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.180
for any future modeling.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:37.660
Now categorical variables
are most commonly

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:41.890
fit by neural networks, which is
why within R many of the most

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.680
common ways of
creating them are using

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:48.550
the keras or tenserflow
for packages or the new torch

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:49.690
package.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:52.690
However, I find the
simplest way to do

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:55.360
it is with the
embed package, which

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.510
is one of the add-on
packages in a tidymodel's

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:02.090
suite of packages.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:06.380
For example, let's say you had
a data set of sales transactions

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:09.950
and you wanted to predict, which
transactions are fraudulent

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:12.180
and which ones are not.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.850
Having product in
there, you can imagine

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:18.390
how that would help the
predictive ability of the model

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:22.260
but with over 4,500
unique products, that's

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:27.030
going to cause problems
with creating your model.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:30.240
But with the embed
package, all you have to do

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:36.150
is add one function step embed
onto a tidymodel's recipe

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.860
to create this embedding.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:41.250
Once you've prepped this
recipe, the embeddings

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:42.330
are there for use.

00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:48.170
And you can see here
how each product is now

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:52.160
represented by an
embedding of four numbers.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.330
And these four numbers are
going to replace the old product

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:59.030
category within the
data going forward.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:02.480
You can now use this for
any downstream model.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.510
It doesn't even have
to be a neural network.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:08.450
You could also use
this if you wanted just

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:12.470
to explore your data further and
learn more about the products.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.840
Maybe you could use
cosine similarity

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:19.500
to figure out which products
are most similar to each other.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.040
Now there's obviously a ton
more that I could talk about

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.570
with categorical embeddings.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:26.990
It's a topic that I
find really exciting.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:28.910
I hope you learned
and discover more

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:32.060
about categorical
embeddings, as you use them

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.040
within your modeling.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.690
Thank you very much for
coming and good luck.

